  --border: rgba(232,241,248,.16);
  --border-strong: rgba(232,241,248,.3);
  --header-bg: #0B1A2F;
  --header-text: #E8F1F8;
  --footer-bg: #091627;
  --shadow: 0 2px 12px rgba(0,0,0,.45);
  --overlay: rgba(4,12,24,.7);
  --on-accent: #06121F;   /* text on bright accent buttons */
  --logo-filter: brightness(0) invert(1) ... 
  --scroll-track:#0B1A2F; --scroll-thumb:#2C4A6E;
}
```

Transitions: `html.theme-transition *` or apply transition on key elements. Spec: 300ms ease transitions on bg/text. I'll add a global rule:
```css
body, #app, .askatek-header, .askatek-footer, ... { transition: background-color .3s ease, color .3s ease, border-color .3s ease; }
```
Simplest robust: 
```css
:root { --theme-transition: background-color .3s ease, color .3s ease, border-color .3s ease, box-shadow .3s ease; }
```
and apply `transition: var(--theme-transition)` to a broad set of selectors. Applying to `*` is bad (breaks transform transitions? it would only add color transitions; but `*` transition would override existing transitions like swiper). Better: apply to a curated list: body, #app, header, footer, nav items, cards, buttons, inputs.

I'll do:
```css
body, #app, .askatek-header, .askatek-nav, .el-menu, .el-menu-item, .el-submenu__title, .askatek-footer, .breadcrumb, .subNav, .shading-menu, .el-button, .el-input__inner, .el-card, .page-component__scroll, h1..h6, p, span, a, li, div { transition: background-color .3s ease, color .3s ease, border-color .3s ease; }
```
Hmm `div` broad — it'd add 300ms color transitions to everything, which is actually what "background color and text color fade together" wants. Risk: elements with hover transitions defined in component CSS will be overridden if the component sets `transition: all .2s` (component CSS wins if it has higher specificity or comes later). Using a rule with low specificity (`:root[data-theme] ...`?) Hmm.

Let me limit to a curated list, verified via screenshots. I'll include div/span? No. Curated:

```css
html, body, #app,
.askatek-head, .askatek-header, .askatek-nav, .el-menu--horizontal, .el-menu-item, .el-submenu__title,
.subNav, .subnav-top-item, .subnav-col, .subnav-list li,
.shading-menu, .shading-nav-link,
.askatek-footer, .askatek-footer-page,
.breadcrumb, .breadcrumb_bg,
.page-component__scroll,
.el-button, .el-input__inner, .el-input-group__append,
.warp, .warp_2, .container, .container_2,
.home, .home-container, .loop-item, .product_item, .product_wrapper, .product_introduce,
.el-card, .el-pagination button, .el-pager li,
.el-form-item__label, .el-table, .el-table th, .el-table td,
[class*="card"], [class*="wrapper"], [class*="container"]
```
The last three attribute selectors are risky/noisy. I'll go with explicit list, and rely on view-level conversion to add `transition: var(--theme-transition)` in their own styles.

Actually simpler and safer: put the transition on `:root` and use
```css
* , *::before, *::after { transition-property: background-color, border-color, color, fill, stroke; transition-duration: .3s; transition-timing-function: ease; }
```
This sets transition-property/duration for all elements. Components that declare their own `transition: ...` shorthand later in the cascade will override it (they're more specific? no — same specificity for `*`, but later in source order wins; component styles injected after main CSS win). But careful: this adds a 300ms color transition on hover states everywhere, making hovers feel slow/laggy (e.g. nav hover fading 300ms). Actually that's a "smooth" feel, mostly fine. But it can make things feel sluggish. Hmm.

Compromise: use `*` rule but only while a `.theme-anim` class is on `:root` — added right before theme switch and removed after 350ms. That's the classic approach: no perf/hover downsides, and guaranteed synchronized 300ms fade during actual theme change. I'll implement that in theme.js: add class `theme-transition` to `<html>` before applying the new theme; remove after 400ms.

```css
html.theme-transition *, html.theme-transition *::before, html.theme-transition *::after {
  transition: background-color .3s ease, color .3s ease, border-color .3s ease, fill .3s ease, stroke .3s ease, box-shadow .3s ease !important;
}
```
Using `!important` during the transition window ensures all elements fade, and it's temporary, so it won't fight other transitions except during those 350ms. Also need to avoid transitioning on initial load (apply theme before paint without the class).

For "follow system" auto mode: on load, if mode is auto, we resolve before paint via inline script? main.js runs after HTML parse; body might flash. To avoid FOUC, I can add a small inline script in public/index.html that reads localStorage and sets `data-theme` + background color on the html element immediately. Let me check public/index.html.

Also need `<html>` background to be themed so no white flash: set `html { background: var(--bg) }`.

Let me check public/index.html and vue.config.js.

