I'll write stubs for about, contact, join, news, product, production, rd, placeholder (8 modules × 2 langs = 16 stub files). Then launch 5 subagents.

Let me write them compactly with a single PowerShell script? Better to use the write tool for each... 16 writes is a lot of calls. I can batch multiple write calls in one message (parallel). Let me do that: 16 write calls in one message. Actually simpler: use one pwsh command to create them all with Set-Content. That's efficient and legitimate (creating new files with known content). But the guidance prefers the write tool for text files. Using pwsh for 16 tiny files is pragmatic. I'll use write tool calls in parallel batches — cleaner and auditable. Let me do 16 in one message.

Actually, to reduce noise: each stub can be `export default {};` — I'll instead have the agents create their own module files and I'll write the index with dynamic content. Since esbuild/webpack requires static imports, stubs are needed. OK, 16 writes.

Hmm — alternative that avoids 16 files: use `require.context` in the index to auto-load all modules in the directory! Webpack supports `require.context('./zh-CN', false, /\.js$/)` and I can build the messages object by filename. But with stub files absent, the dir would be empty → agents create files as they go, and require.context is resolved at build time (all files present after agents finish). This avoids stubs entirely and is elegant:

```js
function loadModules(ctx) {
  const out = {};
  ctx.keys().forEach((key) => {
    const name = key.replace(/^\.\//, "").replace(/\.js$/, "");
    out[name] = ctx(key).default || ctx(key);
  });
  return out;
}
export default loadModules(require.context("./zh-CN", false, /\.js$/));
```
