Excellent! Both look good:
- subNav panel in night mode: deep blue panel, light text, Chinese category names (消费类音频 etc.), accent blue for active/hover. 
- Footer in night: dark navy footer with Chinese headings 快速导航 / 消费类音频 / 工业类音频, links translated, copyright + slogan translated, back-to-top button visible in accent blue.
- The placeholder images (logo1.png, codeqr.png, weix/phone) have their own light backgrounds so they remain visible. Good.
- Note the footer heading "消费类音频" — the group title uses `$label(g)` → term dictionary. 

Issues:
1. In the subNav night screenshot, one item "USB 音频耳机（支持 Teams）" appears highlighted/accent — that's the hover state (mouse was there from the automated hover). Fine.
2. Footer: the `askatek-footer-line` is a light line — ok.
3. The nav "关于 aska" in the footer list — should the footer's fast_navigation items use header.nav keys? Yes they do ($label with i18nKey). It shows 首页/关于 aska/产品中心/研发中心/新闻中心/生产制造/加入我们/联系我们 — home and join us ARE included in the footer (footer uses fast_navigation.childer directly, including isNav items). Consistent with original data.

Now let me fix the flawed test assertion (use title attributes) and re-run to get a full green, then continue with:
- Checking other pages in both themes (about, contact, join, news, product center, rd, production) for contrast issues. This is where problems likely lurk (light-on-light text).
- Then final: build, cleanup (.shots), README documentation.

Let me fix the assertion and add a multi-page dark-mode audit: for each route, screenshot night and check for suspicious low-contrast text (e.g., dark text on dark bg). Automated contrast checking: I can compute, for each visible text element in the page, the effective background color and text color, then flag pairs with contrast < 3.0. That's a powerful audit! Let me implement it in the verification script: a function that walks elements with text, computes contrast ratio against the nearest non-transparent background ancestor, and reports the worst offenders (excluding elements over images/background-image).

That would catch light-on-light issues. Let me implement:

```js
function lum(rgb) { ... }
function contrast(fg, bg) {...}
const offenders = await page.evaluate(() => {
  const parse = (c) => { const m = /rgba?\(([^)]+)\)/.exec(c); if (!m) return null; const p = m[1].split(',').map(s=>parseFloat(s)); return { r:p[0], g:p[1], b:p[2], a: p.length>3?p[3]:1 }; };
  const L = ({r,g,b}) => { const f = (v) => { v/=255; return v<=0.03928? v/12.92 : Math.pow((v+0.055)/1.055, 2.4); }; return 0.2126*f(r)+0.7152*f(g)+0.0722*f(b); };
  const ratio = (a,b) => { const la=L(a), lb=L(b); const hi=Math.max(la,lb), lo=Math.min(la,lb); return (hi+0.05)/(lo+0.05); };
  const effBg = (el) => {
    let node = el;
    while (node && node !== document.documentElement) {
      const cs = getComputedStyle(node);
      if (cs.backgroundImage && cs.backgroundImage !== 'none') return null; // 背景是图片，跳过
      const bg = parse(cs.backgroundColor);
      if (bg && bg.a > 0.9) return bg;
      node = node.parentElement;
    }
    const hb = parse(getComputedStyle(document.body).backgroundColor);
    return hb;
  };
  const out = [];
  document.querySelectorAll('#app *').forEach((el) => {
    if (!el.offsetParent && el.tagName !== 'BODY') return;   // 不可见
    const text = Array.from(el.childNodes).filter(n => n.nodeType===3).map(n=>n.textContent.trim()).join('');
    if (!text) return;
    const cs = getComputedStyle(el);
    const fg = parse(cs.color); if (!fg || fg.a < 0.3) return;
    const bg = effBg(el); if (!bg) return;
    const r = ratio(fg, bg);
    if (r < 4.5) out.push({ text: text.slice(0,40), cls: el.className && el.className.toString().slice(0,60), ratio: Math.round(r*100)/100, fg: cs.color, bg: `rgb(${bg.r},${bg.g},${bg.b})` });
  });
  return out;
